Eight of the nine are uncategorized; the only unused template is <code>Template:BITwiki</code>. <code>Template:Infobox</code> is categorized under <code>Concepts</code> and is used across constitutional, epistemic, ontology, schema, governance, migration and validation documents.

=== The Infobox discrepancy ===
The live <code>Template:Infobox</code> is small: it renders title, type, status, related implementation and category. Later V1 pages pass many additional parameters such as description, author, version, epistemic status, confidence, workflow and ontology. Those parameters express important '''design intent''', but the actual live template does not consume most of them.

V2 must therefore recover the intended schema from the writings without pretending V1 already implemented it.

=== Wanted templates ===
<code>Special:WantedTemplates</code> contains 59 referenced-but-uncreated templates. The largest reference is <code>Template:Documentation</code> (82 references), followed by planned document-header, epistemic-marker, schema-matter, infobox, portal, citation and validation templates.

These references are evidence of intended architecture. They are '''not implemented V1 templates''' and must not be bulk-created in V2 merely to eliminate red links. Each function is evaluated against V2 primitives first.

== Categories: membership without category pages ==
V1 uses '''162 distinct category names''', but the Category namespace contains '''zero created category pages'''. Every V1 category is therefore a membership/index label without authored Category-page documentation.

This is crucial. The categories record useful historical classification decisions, but the intended hierarchy described in [[CATEGORY ARCHITECTURE]] was never fully instantiated as a documented Category namespace.

The archive preserves:
* all 162 names in <code>archive-v1/categories/index.json</code>;
* complete membership for every category in <code>archive-v1/categories/members/</code>;
* member counts and whether an editable Category page existed;
* the generated wanted/unused/uncategorized category reports.

See [[BITwiki:V1 category migration]] for the V2 refactor.

== Semantic implementation ==
V1 contains extensive Main-namespace documentation for properties, entity types, relationships, ontology alignment, SMW templates and Lua modules. The actual namespace implementation is much smaller.

=== Property namespace ===
Only four Property pages exist, all imported vocabulary:
* <code>Property:Foaf:homepage</code>
* <code>Property:Foaf:knows</code>
* <code>Property:Foaf:name</code>
* <code>Property:Owl:differentFrom</code>

The many <code>[[Has ...::...]]</code> annotations in Main pages may create implicit SMW properties, but they are not equivalent to authored <code>Property:</code> definition pages.

=== smw/schema ===
Three pages exist:
* <code>smw/schema:Group:Predefined properties</code>
* <code>smw/schema:Group:Schema properties</code>
* <code>smw/schema:Profile:Facetedsearch default profile</code>

=== Concept / Module ===
No Concept namespace pages and no Module namespace pages exist in the archived public V1 snapshot. Documentation describing concepts/Lua modules must therefore be read as design/specification material unless an implementation exists elsewhere.

== MediaWiki configuration surface ==
Six MediaWiki pages exist:
* <code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code>
* <code>MediaWiki:KnowledgeGraphOptions</code>
* four SMW imported-vocabulary configuration pages for FOAF, OWL, schema and SKOS.

Two File description pages also exist and are archived with their revision text. Binary file preservation is a separate media-export concern from page-source preservation.

== Maintenance state ==
The corrected exhaustive QueryPage snapshot reports:
* 20 uncategorized Main pages;
* 8 uncategorized templates;
* 1 unused template;
* 162 wanted categories;
* 59 wanted templates;
* 888 wanted pages;
* 0 broken redirects;
* 0 double redirects.

The 20 uncategorized Main pages are mostly Book Matter subpages, Component fragments and <code>Test Page</code>. V2 should classify these by function rather than mechanically adding a generic category to silence the report.

== Governing migration rule ==
<pre>
design writing ≠ implemented namespace object
wanted object ≠ implemented object
category membership ≠ documented category page
semantic annotation ≠ authored Property page
specification ≠ deployed capability
</pre>

V2 preserves all five forms of evidence and states which layer a claim refers to.

